--TEST--
`streamAs<T>` walks a result set a row at a time and builds each row into the class its call site wrote, through a connection and through a transaction alike — `rule:core-classes/db-streaming`, `rule:core-classes/derive-attribute`, `rule:classes/no-traits`, spec § 18
--FILE--
<?nvs
// Spec § 18 gives `stream` and `streamAs<T>` one statement, one binding rule
// and one held connection: what differs is that the walk's element is a
// `Core\Db\Row` on the first and the written class on the second. So this case
// asks for the rows a member that buffered could answer just as well, and what
// it pins is that they arrive as `Person`s — the hydration `queryAs` does, done
// one row at a time as the walk reaches each.
//
// SQLite is the backend a case can reach: a `:memory:` block needs no server.
// The four drivers with a wire assert the walk itself from
// `crates/nvs-stdlib/tests/db_stream.rs`.
use Core\Db\Derive;
use Core\Db\Transaction;

#[Derive]
class Person {
    public uint $id;
    // § 9: every column a row yields is `tainted` where its type can carry it,
    // so a field receiving one says so or the call site is a diagnostic.
    public tainted string $name;

    public function constructor(uint $id, tainted string $name)
    {
        $this->id = $id;
        $this->name = $name;
    }
}

var $db = Core\Db::connect("main");
$db->execute("create table people (id integer primary key, name text not null)", []);
$db->executeMany("insert into people (name) values (?)", [["ada"], ["grace"], ["alan"]]);

// The `foreach` binds a `Person` and not a `Core\Db\Row`, which is the whole of
// what the type argument bought: the class is built where the row is read.
foreach ($db->streamAs<Person>("select id, name from people order by id", []) as Person $person) {
    echo "person ", $person->id, " ", $person->name, "\n";
}

// `rule:classes/no-traits` declares the member once, on `Core\Db\Queryable`, so a
// transaction's walk is its connection's: the same statement, the same class,
// through the receiver the closure was handed.
$db->transaction(fn (Transaction $tx): void => {
    foreach ($tx->streamAs<Person>("select id, name from people order by id desc", []) as Person $each) {
        echo "in transaction ", $each->name, "\n";
    }
});
--FILE nvs.toml--
# § 3: a SQLite connection's file path comes from its config block, so
# `db.connect` covers it.
[capabilities.db]
connect = ["main"]

[db.main]
driver = "sqlite"
path = ":memory:"
--EXPECT--
person 1 ada
person 2 grace
person 3 alan
in transaction alan
in transaction grace
in transaction ada
